The nutrient iron is needed to make haemoglobin, which carries oxygen around the body, and is also important for building and maintaining healthy muscles【https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet】. It is found in rich animal sources such as liver, kidney, red meat and sardines, as well as fortified breakfast cereals, and in plant‑based sources including wholegrain cereals fortified with iron, pulse vegetables, dark green vegetables, nuts and dried fruit【https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet-sheet】. Reference intakes are 8.7 mg a day for men aged 19 and over, 14.8 mg a day for women aged 19 to 49, and 8.7 mg a day for women aged 50 and over【https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron】.

